### Subscription-Aware Authentication & Throttling

**apps/whk-wms/src/auth/gqlThrottler.guard.ts (lines 8-27)**

The throttler guard explicitly skips subscription operations since they're persistent connections:

```typescript
@Injectable()
export class GqlThrottlerGuard extends ThrottlerGuard {
  /**
   * Skip throttling for WebSocket subscription operations and when
   * GraphQL context doesn't have HTTP request objects.
   */
  protected async shouldSkip(context: ExecutionContext): Promise<boolean> {
    const nodeEnv = (process.env.NODE_ENV || "").toLowerCase();
    if (nodeEnv === "e2e" || nodeEnv === "test") {
      return true;
    }

    if (context.getType<GqlContextType>() === "graphql") {
      const gqlCtx = GqlExecutionContext.create(context);
      const info = gqlCtx.getInfo();

      // Skip subscription operations
      if (info?.operation?.operation === "subscription") {
        return true;
      }
```

`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`
The WebSocket architecture uses **dual protocols** for flexibility: GraphQL subscriptions handle business-level real-time events (reconciliation updates, comments) via PubSub with optional client-side filtering, while Socket.IO gateways manage lower-level streaming scenarios (progress tracking, AI agent responses) with explicit authentication and session management. The PubSub layer is **environment-aware**, using Redis for distributed deployment and in-memory for local development—this avoids infrastructure complexity during development while maintaining production scalability. Subscription operations are **excluded from rate limiting** since they represent persistent connections, not individual requests.
